{
  "id": "funnel_u_physician_power",
  "reference": "Detectability and Garwood intervals on a physician-scale u funnel: central-line infections per 1000 line-days (multiply=1000) for ten physicians with 180 to 950 line-days. The pooled rate is 5.86 per 1000, yet the first physician needs a true rate of 43.9 per 1000 (7.5x) to be flagged with 80% power, and every physician needs more than a doubling: underpowered is true with n_underpowered 10 of 10 and a median detectable ratio of 5.32. The Poisson algebra is the O/E chart's with exposure in place of expected events, so the u funnel reuses oe_detectable_ratio and oe_point_ci; a physician with zero infections gets a lower bound of exactly 0. min_detectable and the CI scale with multiply like every y-scale column; the ratio median does not. Cross-checked against direct Poisson sums: power 0.8 and tails 0.025 to ~1e-10 (the 1e-9 quantization of the lgamma-based solve). Upper contours sit at the exact one-sided test's boundary, one count beyond the interpolated quantile, so a point is beyond either line exactly when its exact p-value is below that line's tail probability (docs/methods.md \u00a74.1).",
